exercises.solution:
```python
def span(vectors):
    space = {0}
    for v in vectors:
        space |= {s ^ v for s in space}
    return space


def extend_to_basis(start, n):
    basis = list(start)
    for v in range(1, 2 ** n):
        if v not in span(basis):
            basis.append(v)
    return basis
```

This is the proof of Theorem 6 run on a finite space: keep adding vectors outside the span (each addition keeps the list independent) until nothing can be added — the list is then maximal, hence spanning, hence a basis. In a finite space the climb stops after at most $n$ additions; Zorn's lemma is what guarantees a maximal independent set in infinite dimensions.
